  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "$\\,$ $P(z):$ ${e}^{z}<0$ or ${z}^{4}\\le -1.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $R,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $\u2205.$\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $R.$\n- $\u2200z\\in R,\\,P(z).$",
  "worked_solution": "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n\u2200x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${e}^{z}<0$\" is $\u2205,$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${z}^{4}\\le -1$\" is $\u2205.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\u2205\\cup \u2205=\u2205.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ does *not* coincide with the universal set, the statement $\u2200z\\in R,\\,P(z)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
